<!--No citations are required in the article lead per [[MOS:LEADCITE]], as long as the content is cited in the article body, as it should be. Do not add missing-citation tags like {{cn}} to the lead. If necessary, {{not verified in body}} can be used, or the content removed.-->
'''15.ai''' was a free [[non-commercial]] web application and research project that used [[artificial intelligence]] to generate [[Speech synthesis#Text-to-speech systems|text-to-speech]] voices of fictional characters from popular media. Created by a [[pseudonym]]ous artificial intelligence researcher known as [[15 (software engineer)|15]], who began developing the technology as a [[freshman]] during their undergraduate research at the [[Massachusetts Institute of Technology]] (MIT), the application allowed users to make characters from video games, television shows, and movies speak custom text with emotional inflections. The platform was able to generate convincing voice output using minimal training data; the name "15.ai" referenced the creator's statement that a voice could be cloned with just 15 seconds of audio. It was an early example of an application of [[generative artificial intelligence]] during the initial stages of the [[AI boom]].

Launched in March 2020, 15.ai became an [[Internet phenomenon]] in early 2021 when content utilizing it went viral on social media and quickly gained widespread use among [[Fandom|Internet fandoms]], such as the ''[[My Little Pony: Friendship Is Magic]]'', ''[[Team Fortress 2]]'', and ''[[SpongeBob SquarePants]]'' fandoms. The service featured emotional context through [[emoji]]s, precise pronunciation control, and multi-speaker capabilities. Critics praised 15.ai's accessibility and emotional control but criticized its technical limitations in [[prosody (linguistics)|prosody]] options and non-English language support, with mixed results depending on character complexity. 15.ai is credited as the first platform to popularize [[audio deepfake|AI voice cloning]] in [[Internet meme|memes]] and [[content creation]].{{efn|name=memes|In particular, it has been credited as such by the following: ''[[Rock Paper Shotgun]]'',<ref>{{harvnb|Morton|2021}}: "Machine learning is absolutely fascinating and yet I mostly just enjoy when people use impressive tech to create weird skits and memes. That's exactly what everyone appears to be doing with an extremely impressive machine-learning tool that lets you type in text for various characters to say out loud. [...] It's made possible by the text to speech algorithm 15.ai that studies clips of characters and uses deep-learning to make those characters say whatever the heck you want."</ref> ''[[Clubic]]'',<ref>{{harvnb|Lamorlette|2021}}: "Vous avez toujours rêvé de faire dire n'importe quoi à vos personnages de jeux vidéo préférés (ou détestés) ? Le site 15.ai l'a fait !" ({{translation|i=yes}} "Have you always dreamed of making your favorite (or hated) video game characters say anything? 15.ai has finally made it happen!")</ref> ''GLITCHED'',<ref>{{harvnb|Cocomello|2021}}: "However, back then if you wanted to create your own dialogue, it required layers of sound enhancements and tweaks. Thankfully, the world has evolved and now thanks to the 15.ai app, we can make [...] popular characters say whatever we want"</ref> ''[[United Daily News]]'',<ref>{{harvnb|MrSun|2021}}: "大家是否都曾經想像過，假如能讓自己喜歡的遊戲或是動畫角色說出自己想聽的話，不論是名字、惡搞或是經典名言，都是不少人的夢想吧。不過來到 2021 年，現在這種夢想不再是想想而已，因為有一個網站通過 AI 生成的技術"，({{translation|i=yes}} "Have you ever imagined what it would be like if your favorite game or anime characters could say exactly what you want to hear? Whether it's names, parodies, or classic quotes, this is a dream for many. However, as we enter 2021, this dream is no longer just a fantasy, because there is a website that uses AI-generated technology,")</ref> ''Analytics India Magazine'',<ref>{{harvnb|VK|2023}}: "While AI voice memes have been around in some form since '15.ai' launched in 2020, [...]"</ref> ''[[Inverse (website)|Inverse]]'',<ref>{{harvnb|Wright|2023}}: "AI voice tools used to create "audio deepfakes" have existed for years in one form or another, with 15.ai being a notable example."</ref> [[Speechify]],<ref>{{harvnb|Weitzman|2023}}: "It gained popularity because it was the first AI voice platform that featured an assortment of fictional characters from a variety of media sources"</ref> ''[[The Guardian (Nigeria)|The Guardian]]'' (Nigerian newspaper),<ref> {{harvnb|Temitope|2024}}: "During this period, 15.ai earned credit for single-handedly popularizing AI voice cloning—often described as 'audio deepfakes'—in memes, viral content, and fan-driven media."</ref> ''[[Independent Nigeria]]'',<ref>{{harvnb|Abisola|2025}}: "Many credit 15.ai as the first mainstream text-to-speech platform that truly made 'audio deepfakes' go viral,"</ref> and Alex Irpan.<ref>{{harvnb|Irpan|2025}}: "multiple startups credit 15.ai for creating the market they now compete in."</ref>}}

Voice actors and industry professionals debated 15.ai's implications, raising concerns about [[technological unemployment|employment impacts]], [[Audio deepfake#Incidents of fraud|voice-related fraud]], and [[Deepfake pornography|potential misuse]]. In January 2022, it was discovered that a company called [[Voiceverse NFT plagiarism scandal|Voiceverse had generated voice lines using 15.ai without attribution]], promoted them as the byproduct of their own technology, and sold them as [[non-fungible token]]s (NFT) without permission. News publications universally characterized this incident as the company having "stolen" from 15.ai.{{efn|name=stolen|including the following news publications: ''[[The Mary Sue]]'',{{sfn|Lawrence|2022}} ''[[Kotaku Australia]]'',{{sfn|Innes|2022}} ''[[Eurogamer]]'',{{sfn|Phillips|2022}} ''[[NME]]'',{{sfn|Williams|2022}} ''[[:fi:Muropaketti|Muropaketti]]'',{{sfn|Muropaketti|2022}} ''[[Gamereactor]]'',{{sfn|Groth-Anderson|2022}} ''[[Web3 Is Going Just Great]]'',{{sfn|White|2022}} ''[[:ru:StopGame|StopGame]]'',{{sfn|Skorich|2022}} ''[[:ru:iXBT Games|iXBT Games]]'',{{sfn|Piletsky|2022}} ''[[:ru:DTF|DTF]]'',{{sfn|Granger|2022}} ''[[Sport.es]]'',{{sfn|Baylos|2022}} ''[[:sv:FZ|FZ]]'',{{sfn|Myrén|2022}} [[AI Incident Database]],{{sfn|Sonali|2022}} [[AI, Algorithmic, and Automation Incidents and Controversies]],{{sfn|AIAAIC|2022}} ''[[Webster University#Student life|The Journal]]'',{{sfn|Parker|2022}} ''LevelUp'',{{sfn|Rosas|2022}} ''Stevivor'',{{sfn|Wright|2022}} ''PlayStation Universe'',{{sfn|Carcasole|2022}} ''Checkpoint Gaming'',{{sfn|Wakelin-King|2022}} ''Tech Times'',{{sfn|Henry|2022}} ''Mobidictum'',{{sfn|Aktaş|2022}} ''OtakuPT'',{{sfn|Archer|2022}} ''Gamebrott'',{{sfn|Ifram|2022}} ''GameGuru'',{{sfn|Kuchkanov|2022}} ''Shazoo'',{{sfn|Коэн|2022}} and ''Geek Culture'',{{sfn|Toh|2022}}}} The service went offline in September 2022 due to legal issues surrounding [[artificial intelligence and copyright]]. Its shutdown was followed by the emergence of commercial alternatives whose founders have acknowledged 15.ai's pioneering influence in the field of [[deep learning speech synthesis]]. On May 18, 2025, 15 launched '''15.dev''' as the sequel to 15.ai; on May 28, 2026, 15 announced the indefinite closure of 15.dev to focus on founding Artist Alley, a [[fandom]] marketplace catered toward artists.

== History ==
=== Background ===
{{Broader|Deep learning speech synthesis#History}}
{{See also|History of speech synthesis}}
The field of [[speech synthesis]] underwent a significant transformation with the introduction of [[deep learning]] approaches. In 2016, [[DeepMind]]'s publication of the [[WaveNet]] paper marked a shift toward [[neural network (machine learning)|neural network]]-based speech synthesis, which enabled higher audio quality via causal [[convolutional neural network]]s. Previously, [[concatenative synthesis]]&mdash;which worked by stitching together pre-recorded segments of human speech&mdash;was the predominant method for generating artificial speech, but it often produced robotic-sounding results at the boundaries of sentences.{{sfn|Barakat|Turk|Demiroglu|2024}} In 2018, [[Google AI]]'s Tacotron 2 showed that neural networks could produce highly natural speech synthesis but required substantial training data (typically tens of hours of audio) to achieve acceptable quality. When trained on two hours of training data, the output quality degraded while still being able to maintain intelligible speech; with 24 minutes of training data, Tacotron 2 failed to produce intelligible speech.<ref name="Google">{{harvnb|Google|2018}}</ref> The same year saw the emergence of HiFi-GAN, a [[generative adversarial network]] (GAN)-based [[vocoder]] that improved the efficiency of waveform generation while producing high-fidelity speech,{{sfn|Kong|Kim|Bae|2020}} followed by Glow-TTS, which introduced a [[flow-based generative model|flow-based]] approach that allowed for both fast inference and voice style transfer capabilities.{{sfn|Kim|Kim|Kong|Yoon|2020}} Chinese tech companies like [[Baidu]] and [[ByteDance]] also made contributions to the field by developing breakthroughs that further advanced the technology.{{sfn|Temitope|2024}}

=== 2016–2020: Conception and development ===
[[File:15 at Everfree Northwest 2025.png|thumb|left|upright=0.6|[[15 (software engineer)|15]] at [[Everfree Northwest]] in 2025]]
15.ai was conceived in 2016 as a research project in deep learning speech synthesis by a developer known as [[15 (software engineer)|15]] (at the age of 18{{sfn|Abisola|2025}}) during their freshman year at [[Massachusetts Institute of Technology|MIT]] as part of its [[Undergraduate Research Opportunities Program]].{{sfnm|Chandraseta|2021|Li|2021|Temitope|2024}} 15 was inspired by DeepMind's WaveNet paper, with development continuing through their studies as Google AI released Tacotron 2 the following year. By 2019, they had demonstrated at MIT their ability to replicate WaveNet and Tacotron 2's results using 75% less training data than previously required.{{sfn|Temitope|2024}} The name "15.ai" is a reference to the developer's statement that a voice can be cloned with as little as 15 seconds of data.{{sfnm|Phillips|2022|Temitope|2024|Abisola|2025}}

15 had originally planned to pursue a [[PhD]] based on their undergraduate research, but opted to work in the [[Silicon Valley|tech industry]] instead after their [[startup]] was accepted into the [[Y Combinator]] accelerator in 2019. After their departure in early 2020, 15 returned to their voice synthesis research and began implementing it as a [[web application]]. According to a post on [[Twitter|X]] from 15, instead of using conventional voice datasets like [[LJSpeech]] that contained simple, monotone recordings, they sought out more challenging voice samples that could demonstrate the model's ability to handle complex speech patterns and emotional undertones.<ref group="tweet">{{cite tweet |title=The past and future of 15.ai |url=https://x.com/fifteenai/status/1865439846744871044 |user=fifteenai |number=1865439846744871044 |website=[[Twitter]] |access-date=December 19, 2024 |archive-date=December 8, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241208035548/https://x.com/fifteenai/status/1865439846744871044 }}</ref> During this phase, 15 discovered the Pony Preservation Project, a collaborative project started by [[:/mlp/]], the ''My Little Pony'' board on [[4chan]].{{sfn|Branwen|2020}} Contributors of the project had manually trimmed, denoised, transcribed, and emotion-tagged thousands of voice lines from ''My Little Pony: Friendship Is Magic'' and had compiled them into a [[dataset]] that provided ideal training material for 15.ai.{{sfnm|Branwen|2020|Temitope|2024}}

=== 2020–2022: Release and operation ===
