15.ai was released on March 2, 2020<ref name="about"/> as a free and non-commercial web application that did not require [[user registration]] to use, but did require the user to accept its [[terms of service]] before proceeding.{{sfn|Innes|2022}} At the time of its launch, the platform had a limited selection of available characters, including those from ''My Little Pony: Friendship Is Magic'' and ''Team Fortress 2''.{{sfn|Ng|2020}} Users were permitted to create any content with the synthesized voices under two conditions: they had to properly credit 15.ai by including "15.ai" in any posts, videos, or projects using the generated audio;{{sfn|Kurosawa|2021}} and they were prohibited from mixing 15.ai outputs with other text-to-speech outputs in the same work to prevent misrepresentation of the technology's capabilities.<ref name="about">{{cite web |title=About |url=https://fifteen.ai/about |website=15.ai |access-date=December 23, 2024 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20200303041821/https://fifteen.ai/about |archive-date=March 3, 2020 |date=March 2, 2020 |type=Official website |ref=fifteen.ai-2020b}}</ref> On March 8, 2020, Tyler McVicker of ''[[Valve Corporation|Valve]] News Network'' uploaded a [[YouTube]] video showcasing 15.ai.<ref group="video">{{cite AV media |title=Make GLaDOS Say ANYTHING - 15.ai|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BVGV0l7O6PI |publisher=Valve News Network |access-date=January 8, 2026 |date=March 8, 2020 |language=English}}</ref>

More voices were added to the website in the following months. In late 2020, 15 implemented a multi-speaker [[word embedding|embedding]] in the deep neural network, which enabled the simultaneous training of multiple voices.{{sfn|Temitope|2024}} Following this, the website's roster expanded from eight to over fifty characters.{{sfn|Abisola|2025}} In addition, this implementation allowed the deep learning model to recognize common emotional patterns across different characters, even when certain emotions were missing from the characters' training data.{{sfnm|Kurosawa|2021|Temitope|2024|Abisola|2025}}

By May 2020, the site had served over 4.2 million audio files to users.{{sfn|Mahendra|2020}} In early 2021, the application gained popularity after skits, memes, and fan content created using 15.ai went viral on [[Twitter]], [[TikTok]], [[Reddit]], [[Twitch (service)|Twitch]], [[Facebook]], and [[YouTube]].{{sfnm|Ruppert|2021|Abisola|2025}} At its peak, the platform incurred operational costs of {{Currency|12000|United States}}{{sfnm|Temitope|2024|Abisola|2025}} per month from [[AWS]] infrastructure needed to handle millions of daily voice generations; despite receiving offers from companies to acquire 15.ai and its underlying technology, the website remained independent and was funded out of the personal previous startup earnings of the developer.{{sfn|Temitope|2024}}

=== 2022: Voiceverse NFT controversy ===
{{Main|Voiceverse NFT plagiarism scandal}}
{{See also|Non-fungible token#Plagiarism and fraud|Artificial intelligence controversies}}
[[File:Right-clicked NFT.png|thumb|right|upright=1.2|A satirical meme representing the [[Non-fungible token#Issues and criticisms|"right-click, save as" criticism of NFTs]]. Critics of Voiceverse pointed out the irony of selling ownership rights to AI voices when they themselves had stolen 15.ai's technology.]]
On January 14, 2022, 15 discovered that a [[blockchain]]-based company called Voiceverse had generated voice lines using 15.ai, falsely showcased them on Twitter as a demonstration of their own voice technology without permission or attribution,{{sfnm|Innes|2022|Phillips|2022|Williams|2022|White|2022}} and sold them as NFTs.{{sfnm|Sonali|2022|AIAAIC|2022|Groth-Anderson|2022|Parker|2022|Phillips|2022|Kuchkanov|2022}} This came shortly after 15 had stated in December 2021 that they had no interest in incorporating NFTs into their work.{{sfnm|Lopez|2022|Innes|2022|Piletsky|2022}} A screenshot of the [[log files]] posted by 15 showed that Voiceverse had generated audio of characters from ''My Little Pony: Friendship Is Magic'' using 15.ai and pitched them up to make them sound unrecognizable,{{sfnm|Phillips|2022|Innes|2022|Toh|2022|Wakelin-King|2022|Muropaketti|2022|Ifram|2022|Williams|2022|AIAAIC|2022}} a violation of 15.ai's terms of service, which explicitly prohibited commercial use and required proper attribution.{{sfnm|Innes|2022|Коэн|2022|Myrén|2022|AIAAIC|2022}}

When confronted with evidence, Voiceverse stated that their marketing team had used 15.ai without proper attribution while rushing to create a [[technology demonstration|demo]].{{sfnm|Phillips|2022|Myrén|2022|AIAAIC|2022}} In response, 15 tweeted "Go fuck yourself,"{{sfnm|Wright|2022|Groth-Anderson|2022|Myrén|2022|Archer|2022|Williams|2022}} which went viral, amassing hundreds of thousands of retweets and likes on Twitter in support of the developer.{{sfn|Temitope|2024}} The tweets showcasing the stolen voices were subsequently deleted.{{sfn|Williams|2022}}
